\
.\" Dummy definitions of GNU extension macros, in case an-ext.tmac is absent.
.de UR
..
.de UE
.RI \\$1
..
.de MR
.BR "\\$1" "(\\$2)\\$3"
..
\
.TH RENOIRPATCH "1" "@PACKAGE_VERSION@" openrenoir
\
.SH NAME
\
renoirpatch \- add minor coffee-making abilities to C source code
\
.SH SYNOPSIS
\
.B renoirpatch
.RB [ \-\-c++ ]
.RB [ \-Al " | " \-Alx " | " \-Am " | " \-A\- ]
.RB [\-o
.IR out.c ]
.I in.c
.br
.RI "..."
.br
.B cc
.RI [... cc-options ...
.RI ... input-files
.IR out.c ...]
.B -lopenrenoir
[...]
\
.PP
.B renoirpatch
.RB [ \-h " | " \-? " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version ]
\
.SH DESCRIPTION
\
.B renoirpatch
adds minor coffee-making abilities to C (and possibly C++) source code.
It currently looks for string variable definitions of the form
.RS
.BI "register char " ident "[] = \(dq" stuff \(dq
[
.BI \(dq more-stuff \(dq
.RB "...]" ;
.RE
and rewrites them into a compressed form, which are decompressed on use.
\
.SH OPTIONS
\
.TP
.B \-\-c++
Try to parse the input file as C++ rather than C.
\
.TP
.BR \-Al ", " \-Alx ", " \-Am ", " \-A\-
Select the type of source code armouring to use.
\
.RS
.PP
.B \-Al
selects line-number-based armouring (the default).
\
.PP
.B \-Alx
selects a stricter \(em but more brittle \(em form of line-number-based
armouring.
\
.PP
.B \-Am
selects marker-based armouring.  A string to be compressed should be
preceded by a marker string of the form
.RS
.BI "register char const " marker_id "[] = \(dq" marker \(dq
.RB [...] ;
.RE
which will provide the key unscramble the compressed string.
\
.PP
.B \-A\-
disables armouring.
.RE
\
.TP
.BI \-o " out.c"
Write the processed output to
.I out.c
rather than producing a series of diffs on standard output.
\
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-? ", " \-\-help
Display tool usage information and exit.
\
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display tool version information and exit.
\
.SH BUGS
\
There are too many mixed metaphors.
\
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\
.MR axrenoirscan 1 ,
.MR patch 1 ,
.MR spatch 1 ,
.MR cc 1
\
